WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
 Hello and welcome back. I just wanted to give you sort of a sense of the actual physical

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.520
 experience of connecting and communicating and computing in the 60s and the 70s. The

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.560
 70s was when I got my start and the lucky people got to use Teletype like was being

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:23.880
 used in that video and other people used punch cards. You also heard the squealing sound

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:31.200
 of the data being converted into sound and back and forth. The key goal was there would

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.520
 be, if you were lucky, one computer within a 20-mile radius of where you were sitting

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.920
 so you could use a local phone call and you could be on all day and do things all day

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.160
 long. That was really exciting and a lot of fun. This was sort of a common, if you were

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.920
 sort of, today you have a $2,000 laptop, the fancy people had these Teletypes right in

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.080
 their office because then they could conveniently sort of do computation and not have to go

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.560
 somewhere to do that computation. So that's pretty exciting. So dial-up was kind of like

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:10.280
 the way the campus operated. But you could also do data transfer with leased lines. The

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:17.720
 typical thing about leased lines is that the phone company, if it's going between two cities,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.880
 digs a hole and puts a cable in and has some number of copper wires in between those cities

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.040
 - this is back then, of course it's fiber optic now - but back then it was copper and

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.560
 every time you would make a call between those two cities it would have to find a free piece

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.000
 of wire and connect it up so that your call, the audio of your call would go through that

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:45.200
 wire. And if you wanted to, you could pay them to lease one of those pieces of wire.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.880
 It meant that people couldn't use that wire for phone calls. You would sort of take it

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.200
 out of service and if they had 500 wires between two cities and you leased one of them then

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.760
 they had 499 wires. And so it was rather expensive. And really, very importantly, the cost was

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:07.600
 based on distance. A mile versus five miles versus 20 miles versus 100 miles, the cost

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.920
 would kind of go up linear based on distance. And so that's a key thing that distance is

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.840
 important in these very limited resource copper wires where you had to dedicate a wire either

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.220
 to a phone call or to data. And so we ended up evolving in the academic world what we

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.480
 called stored and forward networking with the sole purpose of compromising everything

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:36.400
 so that we would keep our cost that we paid for our phone lines to a minimum. We wanted

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.240
 to communicate in academia with people all around the country and all around the world

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:51.120
 so we had to keep the cost low. And so we would lease a leased line, you know, so here

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.660
 would be us sitting with terminals in our offices and we would use dial up to get connected

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:04.040
 and so this might be a few miles, like one to two miles, one to two kilometers. And that's

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.360
 what you had to do. You had to live near a computer or have an office near a computer

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.480
 and then you would use the local telephone network to dial and to do your work. And then

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.160
 there would be one connection out the back of that computer to another computer that

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.320
 would also have users on it and then other computers with users on it, other computers

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:31.960
 with users on it. So this might, for example, be Michigan. This might be Stanford. Stanford

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.960
 would have a computer, Michigan would have a computer. The Stanford people would be connected

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:41.080
 to the Stanford computer. Stanford would have one little connection out its back end and

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:48.400
 to the rest of the world. So you, sitting here, want to talk to somebody sitting here.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.960
 So this is how it worked. You were sort of always connected. So your data would be inside,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:04:00.120
 you know, you type an email into the computer and then you'd say send that out to my colleague

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:07.200
 at Stanford. And so the problem is, is everyone else was sending. And so let's say, for example,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.440
 that somebody sent a pretty big thing. Big purple circle is what they're sending. And

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.480
 then somebody next to you in the office down sends a kind of medium sized orange circle

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.600
 and they're sitting there being stored in the local computer and then you finally finish

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.840
 typing your email messages and it's kind of a small green circle. And now the problem

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:30.000
 is is you're in a line. The way this worked is the software would keep a queue or a line

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
 of those who were going to use the line and then it would sort of start streaming this

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
 stuff out. And once it started, it just kept doing the same file over and over and over.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.880
 Now if it died, it would just start from the beginning again. And so it takes some time

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.280
 for that thing to move across the network. And then when it would move across the network,

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:52.960
 the next message in line would start to be sent across the network and it would use that

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.040
 link. Come on, finish up. There we go. Okay. It takes a while for these things to move

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:02.640
 across the link. And then finally, and this could be 10 minutes later, finally it was

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.560
 your turn and your stuff would work its way through the network and get to the next computer.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.320
 The problem is is once it's in that computer, you face the same problem because if all these

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:21.720
 messages are going from Michigan, I forgot to hit the button thing, from Michigan to Stanford

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.400
 and they're all on the way, they got to go through all these links because there wasn't

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.080
 a direct connection because the direct connection to Stanford would be very expensive. And so

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:37.400
 we find ourselves in a situation where you're stored, you sat on these computers and if

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:43.220
 there was an outage of a network or say this one, this might be ours. You might be sitting

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:47.560
 on this computer for hours on their disk drive, right? Not even in their memory. You'd be

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.040
 sitting on their disk drive for an hour and then finally this would come back or maybe

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.640
 this computer was down. They'd come back up and then the data would start flowing again.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:02.120
 So it was haphazard. I keep telling you, it was awesome. It was just slow. It was awesome

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:08.400
 but slow. So don't feel sorry for us. We loved it. We enjoyed it. It was great talking to

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.120
 colleagues all over the world. So we ended up with a network architecture that encouraged

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.760
 more hops, which means it encouraged more latency and encouraged the likelihood of getting

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:25.000
 stuck behind something large as it worked its way through the network. So for example,

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.480
 we were going to send a message from East Lansing to say the East Coast somewhere and

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.160
 we would have two connections maybe. We would have a connection between Michigan State University

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.280
 and East Lansing and Ann Arbor and then the data would get stored and forward in Ann Arbor

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:47.280
 and then it would get further sent to Cleveland. And if you think about it, the wire between

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.000
 Ann Arbor and Cleveland goes like this. They probably buried wires under the ground. And

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:01.760
 if we could actually convince Toledo, the cost of the big wire versus the cost of the

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.440
 two shorter wires, the cost of the two shorter wires is almost exactly the cost of the big

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:14.520
 wire because it's not really a big wire. And so if we can convince Toledo to join our little

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:23.520
 club, then we don't increase the cost of our communications because it's about the same.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.360
 The wire length is about the same, but we've got one more school for the same cost. And

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.080
 if you keep doing that and you keep saying, "Oh, well, let's put a school here and put

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:36.480
 a school here and put a school here in the middle of a lake and put a school here in

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:44.480
 the middle of a forest," the economics said that you could connect more schools with effectively

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.360
 fixed cost by shortening the distance every time you made a connection. And sometimes

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:56.360
 they would sort of make two connections. But the way it often worked was from the perspective

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:03.200
 of this whole thing is the data would sort of tend to go up to some central location

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:09.320
 and then kind of fan back out from the central location. And so there wasn't a lot of redundant

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:14.480
 links in this whole situation. So we find ourselves in a situation where we can save

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:21.320
 more money, saving money by just simply adding hops. So in this environment, we had a lot

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.760
 of email. Back in those days, we didn't have a lot of images. Computers, you know, barely

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.800
 could do upper and lower case sometimes. It was impressive when we finally got to upper

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:35.400
 lower case, let alone images. And so for the longest time, everything we did was very text

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:39.420
 oriented. Sometimes we would send small files like programs to each other, but it was either

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:44.280
 text. And these days, those are tiny. It's almost like SMS, right? It's almost like the

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:50.000
 size of an SMS message was the size of a typical mail message back in those days. Images, we

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.600
 didn't have computers that could even display them, and so it hardly mattered. So you focus

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.480
 your life on this one computer within a 20-mile radius, and it had this connection to like

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:03.040
 a snake-like connection that sort of went over hill and dale and to somewhere, and then

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.760
 it finally got to Stanford or wherever your mail had to go. But again, it was awesome

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.480
 to be able to sit at a keyboard and talk to people all over the world, even though it

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:18.960
 took four hours. We didn't send as much email back then. And a number of networks came up.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.800
 One of them that became sort of pretty widely used was a thing called Bitnet, and it was

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:29.580
 where everything kind of came back to. Princeton was one of the main hubs of Bitnet, and it

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:34.200
 worked really well. And there was a way to say, "Oh, I need one more school," and we'd

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.120
 go find the ones that are closest, and we'd make the connection. And so that's pretty

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:47.120
 much how the average academic saw networking, one on-campus computer and sort of this weird,

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:53.280
 slow, soaring-forward networking that worked well enough. But during that exact same time,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.560
 from the 1960s to the 1980s, the U.S. Department of Defense was investing in a research network

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.320
 called ARPANET.

